Namaste, to all beings, the sustainer of all powers. We, who are engaged in the work of the world, have taken refuge in you. 48. Meaning of the word

Namah - Namaste; Te-to you; Sarva- all; Bhut- of living beings; Atman- Hey Atma; Sarva- all; Shakti- of powers; Dhar- Hey
Holders; Avyaya- Hey Avyaya; Vishwa- of the universe; Karman- Hey maker; Namah- Salutations; Te- to you; Astu- be;
Tvam- to you; Vayam- we; Sharanam- to take refuge; Gatah- have come..

Oh soul of all beings, oh one who holds all powers, oh imperishable of the universe,
Creator, we bow to you and, while bowing, take refuge in you. Meaning: The Kauravas clearly realized that their lives and destiny were in the hands of God. Srisuka said:
And, having surrendered, with a mind troubled, with a mind full of pride.
He who is blessed with grace, is very auspicious, and is fearless. 49. Meaning of the word:

Shri-Shuka said—Shukadeva Goswami said; Evam—In this way; Prapannaih—By those who surrender; Samvignaih—Extremely sorrowful;
Vepaman—Being agitated; Ayanaih—Having houses; Balah—Lord Balarama; Prasaditah—Became peaceful; Su—Extremely; Prasannah—Peaceful and gentle;
Ma Bhaishtha—Do not be afraid; Iti—Saying thus; Abhayam—Freedom from fear; Dadau—Gave. Shukdev Goswami said: Those Kauravas whose city was faltering and who were in great distress,
were coming to their refuge, upon being praised by such Kauravas, Balram became calm
and became merciful towards them. He said, "Don’t panic." Then their fear was removed. Duryodhana, the hunter, the elephant, and the lion. Dadau cha dwadashashatanyayutani turangaman. 50>. Six thousand chariots, with the sun as their ornament.
Thousands of servants, without any fault, are devoted to their sons. 51. Meaning of the words:

Duryodhana—Duryodhana; Pari-barham—as dowry; Kunjaran—elephants; Shashti—sixty; Haya-nan—years of age; Dadau—given;
Cha—and; Dvadash—twelve; Shatani—hundred; Ayutani—ten thousand; Turangaman—horses; Ratha-nam—of chariots; Shat-sahasrani—six hundred;
Raukmaan—of gold; Surya—like the sun; Varchasam—agile; Daasinam—of maids; Nishka—a necklace studded with jewels;
Kantheenam—in whose necks; Sahasram—one thousand; Duhitru—for his daughter; Vatsalah—full of paternal affection..

Duryodhana, extremely affectionate towards his daughter, gave her 1,200 sixty-year-old elephants as dowry,
